00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:25.120
Remember, we covered the central bank rate cycle yesterday. Today's decision moves that arc forward by keeping the timing of any next move open while tariff effects fade.

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:34.760
Debit spreads let investors express a directional view with defined risk by buying one option and selling another at a different strike in the same expiration.

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:50.400
Multiple unrelated methods are flagging financial names such as BRK slash B, BAC, MUFG, SMFG, and SPGI on trend relative strength and sector rotation at the same time.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:57.200
This convergence reduces reliance on any single signal and shows where capital is rotating into the sector.

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:03.860
Screen for names clearing on at least two independent methods then structure the spread according to your outlook.

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:09.080
Mild moves favor in the money strikes while larger moves favor out of the money strikes.

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:16.120
The approach has historically performed best when volume confirms the rotation rather than when the signal stands alone.

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:22.100
Risk comes from the spread still losing the full net debit if the thesis fails before expiration.

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:32.080
A trader might buy the 42 call and sell the 44 call on BAC for a net debit of $1.20, capping both risk and reward.

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:37.300
The options play analysis stresses that there is no universally better strike choice.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:41.980
The decision hinges on the investor's specific outlook on the underlying stock.

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:54.280
When four separate methods converge on the same sector on one day, the probability of a sustained move increases, but only if volume participation follows through above average levels.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:03:04.120
Canadian investors can replicate this screen inside interactive brokers or quest trade by pulling up relative strength charts and sector ETF flows for the same names.

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:12.060
This is a simulated trade for educational purposes only. No real money is involved. This is not financial advice.

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:18.320
Today's pick is none. We are monitoring BAC on the NYSE in the financial sector.

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:28.100
The strategy is a valuation screen on names showing sector rotation signals across multiple methods with a five session hold period from any eventual entry.

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:36.400
Invalidation occurs if BAC closes below its 50 day moving average on the daily chart with volume above the 20 day average.

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:42.780
The catalyst is multiple independent methods aligning on financials without a single driver dominating.

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:53.620
The technical setup requires names clearing on the daily time frame with volume confirmation required per existing rules and nearest support sits at the 50 day moving average.

00:03:53.620 --> 00:04:05.300
Risk assessment keeps maximum acceptable loss capped at a $1,000 position size with a modest weekly target range of plus 2% to plus 5% if rotation continues.

00:04:06.020 --> 00:04:16.580
Confidence sits at medium because the setup shows alignment across methods but lacks the volume spike required by current rules, so position size stays at zero until confirmation.

00:04:16.580 --> 00:04:27.040
This exercise demonstrates how to wait for volume confirmation before entering a rotation driven name, exactly the constraint required after recent catalyst failures.

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:41.460
The regime check on the last 10 closed trades shows median matched window alpha of negative 1.76%. Confirming the need to raise the bar and avoid any entry until three or more independent factors line up.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:49.420
We simulated no entry on BAC today and will continue monitoring for the required volume spike before committing the position.

