Portfolio performance shows 60 total trades at a 52% wind rate for cumulative P&amp;L of $299.63.

00:06:52.900 --> 00:07:00.400
The matched window alpha versus NASDAQ stands at negative 4.76% across nine rules-based trades.

00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:11.600
Average return per trade is plus 0.50%, with the best trade at plus 20.11%, and the worst at negative 11.80%.

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:20.520
The current streak is one loss. Imagine you own a Canadian bank stock in your TFSA and want to generate income without selling shares.

00:07:21.020 --> 00:07:34.340
You open the options chain for the nearest monthly expiration and see the bid ask spread on the 45 call is 0.35-0.45, while the 40 put shows 0.60-0.70.

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:43.040
Market makers widen spreads when volume is thin or volatility is elevated, so your limit order at the midpoint may not fill immediately.

00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:50.640
Delta tells you how much the option moves with the stock, and Theta shows daily time decay working in your favor on a covered call.

00:07:51.360 --> 00:08:02.320
The concrete misconception to avoid is treating the last traded price as the executable price. Always use the live bid ask and size only what you are willing to have called away at the strike.

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:15.240
An options chain is simply a table listing all available strike prices and expiration dates for a given underlying, with columns for bid, ask, last price, volume, open interest, and the Greeks.

00:08:16.100 --> 00:08:30.440
For a covered call on a name like Royal Bank of Canada, you would first own 100 shares, then scroll to the call side of the chain and locate the strike above the current price that offers the highest premium relative to the probability of being assigned.

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:47.680
Delta on that call might read 0.35, meaning the option price changes by roughly 35 cents for every dollar move in the stock, while Theta of negative 0.08 indicates you collect 8 cents of time decay each day the position remains open.

00:08:48.680 --> 00:08:57.200
Volume and open interest columns reveal liquidity. Anything below 100 contracts per strike often produces wider spreads and poorer fills.

00:08:57.980 --> 00:09:09.540
Professionals always check the implied volatility rank first to confirm the premium is elevated before selling, because a low IV environment shrinks the income component that makes the strategy attractive.

00:09:10.540 --> 00:09:22.000
When you place the order, the brokerage shows the net credit received and the maximum profit if the stock closes above the strike at expiration, turning the abstract chain into a concrete income calculation.

00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:33.400
The same chain also displays the corresponding put side, allowing you to compare a cash secured put strategy that would obligate you to buy the shares at the lower strike if the stock declines.

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:50.600
Because TFSA accounts treat options premiums as tax-free income when the position expires worthless, many Canadian investors use the chain to identify the 30-45-day expirations that historically deliver the best risk-adjusted yield on blue chip holdings.

00:09:51.020 --> 00:10:01.520
Always verify that the strike you select aligns with your fundamental view of the company, so that assignment, if it occurs, still leaves you with a position you are comfortable owning long term.

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:14.680
Open your brokerage platform, pull up the 50-day moving average on BAC, and set a price alert 2% below that level, so you receive notification the moment the invalidation threshold is approached.

